Abstract
Metal-organic framework (MOF)-based electrode materials have become a hot subject for supercapaitors. Herein, Ni-MOFs grown on Co nanoparticles modified carbon nanofibers (CNFs) (C-Co@MOF) are prepared via a facile process. Interestingly, the presence of Co nanoparticles in CNFs not only boosts the hybridization of CNF and MOFs, but also releases Co ions to participate in the growth of MOF, leading to a favorable electrochemical behavior. In detail, the specific capacitance of C-Co@MOF reaches $1201.6\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ that exceeds those of C-M@MOFs $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Ni}$ , V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu and Zn) and CNF@MOF. More importantly, an asymmetric solid-state supercapacitor is assembled using C-Co@MOF and nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes derived from polyaniline as positive and negative electrode materials, respectively, representing a high energy density of 37.0 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ and outstanding durability. This work highlights the superiority of electrospun CNFs modified by metal nanoparticles for the growth of MOF, showing great potential for electrochemical energy storage and conversion applications.
Keywords: metal-organic framework; carbon nanofibers; electrospun; supercapacitor; electrode materials
## Introduction
Focusing on the environmental and resource crises caused by the high dependence on fossil fuels, there is a strong demand for clean and renewable energy in future energy supply. As a consequence, the development of energy conversion and storage has attracted continuous attentions. Supercapacitor plays a key role in electrical energy storage systems, due to its fast charge-discharge rate, long cycle life, and high power density.1, 2 Nevertheless, the low energy density (E) of supercapacitors which are correlated with the specific capacitance (C) and voltage window (V) $_{\mathrm{E=}0.5}$ CV2) limit their broad applications. Facing such challenges, various types of electrode materials are designed to improve charge storage capacity. Additionally, asymmetric solid-state supercapacitors are also constructed to widen the voltage window. According to energy storage mechanisms, electrode materials can be divided into electric double-layer capacitance (EDLC)-type electrode materials, pseudo-capacitance-type electrode materials, and battery-like electrode materials.3-5 The charge storage of EDLC-type electrode materials relies on electrostatic accumulation of surface charge, exhibiting good stability and electrical conductivity. Both pseudocapacitance-type electrode materials and battery-like electrode materials, involving in high capacitance, store charges through reversible redox reactions, which can be distinguished in the shape of electrochemical curves. Integrating different materials makes a positive contribution to high-performance electrode material via complementary advantages.
Recently, metal organic framework (MOF) based electrode materials have displayed promising electrochemical behaviors for supercapacitor, due to the high redox activities originated from metal centers, the large specific surface areas and suitable pore structures provided by the framework, along with dual role as precursor and template.6-8 In general, MOF-based electrode materials can be divided into three categories: (1) Bare MOFs. Mixed metal centers usually lead to a higher pseudocapacitance than single one. (2) MOF-derived materials, such as, carbon materials,9, 10 carbon/metal oxides,11-14 carbon/metal sulfide,15, 16 etc. The architecture and composition of MOFs endow their derivatives with ideal electron and ion transport channels, as well as rich sources (e.g. carbon, metal, nitrogen, phosphorus, sulfur). (3) MOF-based composites. For example, ZIF-67/polyaniline (PANI)/carbon cloth,17 Ni MOF/reduced graphene oxide (rGO),18 MOFs/COFs,19 Mn-MOF/carbon nanotube (CNT),20 Ni-MOF/CNT,21 Ni-MOF/polyacrylonitrile nanofiber,22 etc. have been widely studied as efficient supercapacitor electrodes. Especially, carbon materials can not only compensate the inferior electrical conductivity and stability of MOF, but also can modulate their morphology, contributing to favorable electrochemical behaviors. However, the hydrophobicity of carbon materials makes it difficult for MOF to grow uniformly on the surface.
Carbon nanofibers (CNFs), with the merits of good electrical conductivity, high specific surface area and large aspect ratio, are attractive candidates for the growth of MOF, which can be produced by a vapor-phase synthetic approach, solid-phase route, and electrospinning technique, etc. Among those, electrospinning technology can easily realize the hybridization of CNFs with other components, thereby enabling CNFs with many distinct aspects, including wettability, electrochemical activities, and graphitization degree.23-25 Interestingly, when metal salts as precursors are introduced into CNFs, they can provide corresponding metal ions in a specific environment, which will affect the growth of the functional materials on their surface.26
Inspired by above issues, to achieve a high-performance MOF-based electrode material, different metal or metal oxide (M or MO, $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{}0$ , Ni, V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu, and Zn) nanoparticles encapsulated in CNFs were selected for the growth of MOF, which can be achieved via an electrospinning and calcination followed by a solvothermal reaction. In a typical example, Co nanoparticles decorated CNFs are utilized as supports to grow MOFs with a core-shell structure (C-Co@MOF), which showed a high specific capacitance of 1201.6 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (specific capacity of $600.8\mathrm{~C~}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ) at 0.5 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and an acceptable rate performance. Then, an asymmetric solid-state supercapacitor was built using C-Co@MOF and N-doped CNT derived from PANI nanotube as positive and negative electrode, respectively, which exhibited a high energy density of 37.0 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ at the power density of 2250.2 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ and an excellent cycling performance. This work paves the way to design high-performance MOF-based electrode materials for supercapacitors.
## Materials and methods
Chemicals and materials
All chemical reagents were directly used without further purification. Cobalt acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and nickel acetate tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(Ni(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Manganese acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and copper acetate monohydrate $(\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Ac}_{)2}{\bullet}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ were produced by Tianjin Huadong Reagent Company. Zinc acetate dehydrate $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ was bought from Xilong Scientific Co., Ltd. Terephthalic acid $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , L), iron acetylacetonate $({\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{acac})_{3})$ , vanadium acetylacetonate $\left(\mathrm{V}(\mathrm{acac})_{3}\right)$ and polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) were provided by Aladdin. Molybdenyl acetylacetonate $(\mathrm{Mo}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ ) was purchased from Sigma-aldrich, Co., Ltd. Ammonium persulfate $((\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}.$ , APS), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF), ethanol (EtOH) and potassium hydroxide (KOH) were supplied by Beijing Chemical Works. Nickel nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{(Ni(NO_{3})_{2}{\bullet6}H_{2}O)}$ was provided by Tianjin Tiantai Fine Chemicals Co., Ltd. Polyacrylonitrile (PAN, $\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{w}}{=}80000$ ), and polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, $\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{w}}{=}77000$ ) were obtained from Jilin Chemical Plant, and Beijing Yili Fine Chemical Co., Ltd., respectively. The water involved in this experiment was procured from Wahaha Co., Ltd.
Preparation of C-M $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{M=Co}}$ , Ni, V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu, Zn) and CNFs
Co-CNFs were prepared through a traditional electrospinning and calcination process. Firstly, PAN was dissolved into DMF at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with magnetic stirring. After cooling to room temperature, $\mathrm{Co(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added into the above mixture with a $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ :PAN:DMF) mass ratio of 10:10:90 to obtain the spinning solution which was transferred to a glass capillary for electrospinning. Then, the membrane was pre-oxidized at $240^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ in air and carbonized at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for another $2\mathrm{~h~}$ in Ar with a hating rate of $2\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min^{-1}}$ to produce Co nanoparticles decorated CNFs which was denoted as C-Co. Under the same conditions, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , V(acac)3, Mo(acac)2, $\mathrm{Mn(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , Fe(acac)3, $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and $Z_{\mathrm{n(Ac)}_{2}\bullet2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ were substitutes for $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to achieve products which were marked as C-Ni, C-V, C-Mo, C-Mn, C-Fe, C-Cu, and C-Zn, respectively. The sample obtained without metal salts refers to CNFs.
Preparation of C-M ( $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{}0$ , Ni, V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu, Zn)@MOF and CNF@MOF
A solvothermal synthesis method was carried out for the growth of MOF on C-Co. Typically, $8~\mathrm{mg}$ of C-Co was immersed into the solution including DMF (25 mL), $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO})_{3}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1.164 g) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\left(0.168\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Afterward, the mixture wastransferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling, cleaning and drying, the sample was collected and named as C-Co@MOF. Similarly, C-Co was replaced by CNF, C-Ni, C-V, C-Mo, C-Mn, C-Fe, C-Cu, and C-Zn to obtain the samples designated as CNF@MOF, C-Ni@MOF, C-V@MOF, C-Mo@MOF, C-Mn@MOF, C-Fe@MOF, C-Cu@MOF, and C-Zn@MOF, respectively.
## Preparation of N-CNT
Nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes were derived from PANI nanotubes, and PANI nanotubes were prepared via a template method. First, PAN nanofibers $\left(50\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and aniline $(20~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were dispersed in 1 M HCl ( $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ) aqueous solution for 1 h (ice bath). Second, APS $\mathrm{\Delta40~mg)}$ dissolved in 1 M HCl ( $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was added into the above mixture for $4\mathrm{{h}}$ with stirring. After centrifugation, the sample was cleaned using water and ethanol, and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. The core-shell PAN $@$ PANI and water (40 mL) were added into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to remove the core, forming the PANI nanotubes. Finally, N-doped CNT (N-CNT) was produced by calcination of PANI nanotubes at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ with a heating rate of 2 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## Electrochemical measurements
Three-electrode and two-electrode systems were applied to investigate the electrochemical performance of as-prepared electrode. The three-electrode system consists of a reference electrode $\mathrm{(Hg/HgO)}$ , a counter electrode (Pt foil) and a working electrode. As for the fabrication of working electrode, active material, carbon black, and PTFE with a mass ratio of 8:1:1 were mixed together with appropriate amount of ethanol, which was evenly smeared on Ni foam. About $3~\mathrm{mg}$ of active material was loaded on each electrode. After drying and pressing, the working electrode can be used for test. The fabrication of electrode in two-electrode system is similar to the above working electrode, only the carbon cloth as current collector was selected to replace Ni foam. And meanwhile, PVA-KOH was utilized for the solid-state electrolyte in this system. In detail, PVA was dissolved in water with stirring at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . KOH $(3~\mathrm{mg})$ in water ( $\mathrm{10~mL}$ ) was slowly added into the above solution to obtain the PVA-KOH gel. The positive and negative electrode can be separated by the solid PVA-KOH. The asymmetric device was fixed with raw tape and sealing tape.
## Characterization
A field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, FEI Nova NanoSEM) at $15\mathrm{KV}$ and a transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL JEM-1200 EX) at 100 KV were performed to investigate the morphology of products. Energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) mapping analysis was provided by a FEI Tecnai G2 F20 electron microscope. X-ray diffraction (XRD, PAN-alytical B.V. Empyrean) with Cu Kα radiation, Fourier transform infrared (FTIR, Bruker Vector 22 Spectrometer), Raman spectra (Horiba Lab RAM HR Evolution), and X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS, Thermo Scientific ESCALAB250) were used to explore the chemical structure and composition of composites. The electrochemical performance tests of samples were offered by a CHI660E electrochemical workstation (Shanghai Chenhua instrument Co., Ltd.) and a Land Battery workstation (Wuhan Land Instrument Company, China).
## Results and discussion
The synthetic route of core-shell C-Co@MOF is exhibited in Scheme 1, which mainly includes two steps. First, electrospun $\mathrm{PAN/Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\bullet}6H_{2}O}$ nanofibers were converted into CNFs embedded with Co nanoparticles via a carbonization process in Ar. Second, a typical solvothermal reaction process is performed to in situ growth of MOF on the surface of C-Co nanofibers. During this process, a part of Co ions were released from cobalt nanocrystals because of etching by the deprotonated $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ provided by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},^{27}$ and meanwhile these Co ions as well as Ni ions coordinate with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , producing the Co-doped Ni MOF nanosheets on C-Co nanofibers.
detailed morphology of the as-prepared samples. The SEM image of CNF@MOF (Figure 1a) displays flower-like MOF nanosheets grown on CNFs. As shown in Figure 1b and 1c, compared with CNF, C-Co and C-Ni are covered with the thicker and denser MOF nanosheets, indicating the enhanced adhesion between substrate and MOF due to the modification of Co and Ni nanoparticles. The diameter of C-Co@MOF reaches $2.5{-}3.5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , and meanwhile its interwoven nanosheets with $20{-}50\ \mathrm{nm}$ generate a hierarchically porous and interconnected network, leading to enlarged surface areas and exposed electrochemical active sites. Because of the thick MOFs grown on the surface of CNFs, there is no obvious core-shell structure identified in the TEM image of C-Co@MOF (Figure 1d). Figure 1e-1i show the HAADF-STEM images and EDX elemental mapping of C-Co@MOF. C, O, Ni, and Co elements are detected throughout the sample. From the color depth in both Ni and Co mapping, it can be inferred that the sample possesses a core-shell structure and Co ions participate in the growth of Ni MOF during the solvothermal process. Figure 2a-2f display the SEM images of C-V@MOF, C-Mo@MOF, C-Mn@MOF, C-Fe@MOF, C-Cu@MOF and C-Zn@MOF samples, respectively. The morphologies of C-V@MOF, C-Mn@MOF and C-Cu@MOF are similar to that of CNF@MOF (Figure S1), only C-Mn is covered with denser "flowers". On the other hand, uniform Ni MOF shell composed of tens of irregular nanosheets arrays are covered on the surface of C-Fe and C-Zn nanofibers. Unlike them, C-Mo is covered by a thin Ni MOF film. These various morphologies reveal that the existence of metal nanoparticles in CNFs shows a significant effect on the growth of Ni MOF, including its morphology, size and thickness.
XRD patterns were employed to identify the crystal structure of the as-produced samples. As illustrated in Figure S2, after calcination process, $\mathrm{Co(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , Ni(Ac)2•4H2O,V(acac)3, Mo(acac)2, $\mathrm{Mn(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , Fe(acac)3, $\mathrm{Cu(Ac)}_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in PAN are converted into Co, Ni, $\mathrm{VO}_{1.27}$ , MoC, $\mathrm{{Mn}_{x}\mathrm{{O}}}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ , Cu, respectively. In particular, there is only one group of carbon peaks in the XRD pattern of $\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{Zn}$ , which is owing to the release of Zn species at a high temperature.28 When MOFs are grown on CNFs, C-Co and C-Ni, the three products present similar diffraction peaks (Figure 3a), which are in accord with that of Ni MOF in the previous report.22 As seen from Figure 3b, the XRD patterns of C-V@MOF, C-Mo@MOF, C-Mn@MOF, C-Fe@MOF and C-Cu@MOF also illustrate similar characteristic peaks, and while the peak position of C-Zn@MOF shows obvious shifts. These results indicate that except for Zn, CNFs modified with the other metals (Ni, Co, V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu) do not affect the coordination of nickel with the ligands. Furthermore, the FTIR spectrum of C-Co@MOF was provided to characterize the coordination mode of Co doped Ni MOF. As exhibited in Figure 3c, the intense bands situated at 1585 and $1385~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ are separately ascribed to asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes of -COO groups, whose differences reveal that a bidentate coordination mode exists in the coordination of Ni or Co ions with ligands.29 The stretching vibrations of paraaromatic C-H, and OH- groups correspond to the band at 1500 and $3596\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ , respectively.30
XPS analysis was performed to explore the elemental composition of samples and their surface electronic state. The full survey spectra of C-Co, CNF@MOF and C-Co@MOF in Figure 3d reflect the diversities of their elemental compositions. C-Co@MOF consists of C, O, Ni and Co element. There is no signal of Ni for C-Co sample and there is also no signal of Co in the spectrum of CNF@MOF sample, respectively. From the high resolution spectra of Ni 2p in CNF@MOF and C-Co $@$ MOF (Figure 3e), it can be noticed that the spin-energy separation between Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ is $17.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ , indicating that nickel ions in both samples belong to a bivalent oxidation state.31 Furthermore, after doping by Co, the binding energy of Ni 2p presents a little shift to the lower level. Because of low content, the Co 2p spectrum collected from C-Co@MOF in Figure S3a was mainly fitted with four peaks, including the characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Co}~2\mathbf{p}_{1/2}$ located at $797.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ centered at 781.1 eV and two satellite peaks, which reveal the existence of $C\mathbf{o}^{2+}$ .32, 33 Figure S3b displays the Co 2p spectrum of C-Co sample. Two peaks with the binding energy of 780.18 and $795.95\ \mathrm{eV}$ demonstrate both $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ in C-Co, suggesting that $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ are easier to coordinate with ligands compared to $\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ .34 As shown in figure 3f, the O 1s peak is deconvoluted into two species, corresponding to the -OH $(531.8\ \mathrm{eV})$ and absorbed water (532.8 eV).35, 36 Their positions also display shifts toward a lower binding energy, which can be due to the introduction of Co ions in Ni MOF. In addition, the Co-O bond with the binding energy of $529.8~\mathrm{eV}$ is observed in the O 1s spectrum of C-Co (Figure
S3c). These results confirm the successful preparation of Co-doped Ni MOF on Co embedded CNFs, and reflect the surface electronic state change caused by Co doping.
To testify the positive influence of C-Co as substrate on electrochemical performance of samples as electrode materials for supercapacitor, the CV, GCD, and EIS spectra of C-Co@MOF, C-Ni@MOF and CNF@MOF were illustrated in Figure 4. In detail, Figure 4a shows the CV curves of three samples measured at $20~\mathrm{mV~s^{-1}}$ . A pair of redox peaks arise in each CV curve, corresponding to the reversible redox reaction between active materials and electrolytes, which can be expressed by the following equation. $\mathrm{Ni_{3}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{2}+O H^{-}\leftrightarrow\N i_{3}(O H)_{3}(H_{2}O)(L)_{2}+H_{2}O+e^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Ni_{3-x}C o_{x}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{2}+O H^{-}\leftrightarrow N i_{3-x}C o_{x}(O H)_{3}(H_{2}O)(L)_{2}+H_{2}O+e^{-}}$ Based on the previous reports and the shape of CV curves, this kind of MOF can be classified as battery-type electrode materials. Thus C-M@MOF( $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{}0$ , Ni, V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu, Zn)@MOF and CNF@MOF belong to an EDLC-type and battery-like hybrid electrode material,37, 38 and the energy storage capacity will be discussed from two perspectives, including specific capacitance and specific capacity, which are obtained through the following formula and the results in the Figure 4b.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{C_{\mathrm{sp}}}\mathbf{=}\mathrm{(I}\ \Delta\mathrm{t)}/\mathrm{(m}\ \Delta\mathrm{V)}}\\ {\mathbf{C_{\mathrm{sc}}}{=}\mathrm{(I}\ \Delta\mathrm{t)}/\mathrm{m}}\end{array}
$$
$\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{sp}}}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{sc}}$ refer to the specific capacitance $\left(\mathrm{F~g^{-1}}\right)$ and specific capacity $\big(\mathrm{C~g^{-1}}\big)$ of single electrode, respectively. I, $\Delta$ t, m, and $\Delta\mathrm{V}$ separately represent the discharge current (A), discharge time (s), mass of active materials (g), and potential range (V) in the three-electrode system. As calculated, the specific capacitances of C-Co@MOF, C-Ni@MOF and CNF@MOF are calculated to be 1201.6, 907.3, and 601.1 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{A}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the corresponding specific capacities are 600.8, 453.6, and $300.6\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . These values are in consistent with the areas enclosed by CV curves. In comparison with $\mathrm{C-Ni}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ and CNF@MOF, C-Co@MOF possesses better electron storage capacity, which may be attributed to the uniform loading of Ni MOF and the introduction of Co from inside to the surface of MOF. The specific capacitance of C-Co was calculated to $114.5\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at 0.5 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 4S), which is well below that of ${\mathrm{C-Co}}\textcircled{a}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ . Furthermore, the specific capacitance of $\mathrm{C-Co}@\mathrm{MOF}$ is also higher than many other MOF-based electrode materials, for example, rGO-Ni-doped MOF composite $(758~\mathrm{~F~}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in $1\ \mathrm{~M~}\ \mathrm{KOH})$ ;39 Ni-MOF/rGO (954 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ );18 Co/Fe-MOF $(319.5\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\ \mathrm{A}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1});{^{40}\mathrm{rGO}}/\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ derived from GO/MOF composites (869.2 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ );41 PPNF@Co-Ni MOF (1096.2 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ );42 CNTs@Mn-MOF (203.1 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ).20 The CV curves at different scan rates of C-Co@MOF are illustrated in Figure 4c. When the scan rate increases from 5 to 100 $\mathrm{\mV\s^{-1}}$ , the symmetric shapes of CV curves remain unchanged, exhibiting the superior reversibility of redox reaction. And meanwhile the redox peak positions shift to respective sides owing to inadequate diffusion reaction caused by the increased internal diffusion resistance at higher scanning rates. As depicted in Figure 4d, the charge-discharge plateaus in GCD curves are related to the redox reactions. In addition, according to the specific capacitance at different current densities, the corresponding capacitance retention can be calculated and shown in Figure 4e. When the current density increases from 0.5 to 15 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the specific capacitance of C-Co@MOF, C-Ni@MOF and CNF@MOF remain the initial $45.38\%$ , $48.80\%$ , and $29.80\%$ , respectively. From the results, it can be inferred that the embedded Co and Ni nanoparticles in the CNFs contribute to their improved rate performance.
EIS measurements of C-Co@MOF, C-Ni@MOF and CNF@MOF samples are further employed to study their resistance characteristics. The radius of semicircle in the high-frequency region, the slope of straight line in low-frequency region, and the intercept of horizontal axis represent charge transfer resistance, diffusion resistance and intrinsic resistance, respectively. As illustrated in Figure 4f, C-Co@MOF possesses lower charge transfer resistance and diffusion resistance than both C-Ni@MOF and CNF@MOF electrode, which benefits from the Co doping in Ni MOF. The resistance characteristics of three electrodes correspond to the differences in their electrochemical properties, which is strongly correlated with their structure and composition. First, the hierarchical core-shell structure combined with porous MOF provides large specific surface areas and expose abundant active sites, promoting the diffusion of ions and redox reaction. Second, the introduction of Co nanoparticles into CNFs not only catalyzes the graphitization of carbon which accelerates the electron transport but also supplies the Co species for doping in Ni MOF.43-45 In addition, more MOF nanosheets grow on C-Co compared with those on pure CNFs. Third, Ni MOF itself displays ideal pseudocapacitive behaviors, whose electrochemical activities are further improved by Co doping, leading to good capacitive performance.
To acquire in-depth insights into the influence of different kinds of metal encapsulated in CNFs on the electrochemical behaviors, the electrochemical measurements (CV, GCD, and EIS) of C-M MOF $\mathrm{M}{=}\mathrm{V}$ , Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu, and Zn) samples have been shown in Figure 5. Among them, the CV curve at $10\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ of C-Mn@MOF shows the largest enclosed area, indicating its highest specific capacitance. By calculating, the specific capacitances of C-V@MOF, C-Mo@MOF, C-Mn $@$ MOF, C-Fe@MOF, C-Cu@MOF, and C-Zn@MOF is 677.7, 632.0, 786.3, 777.3, 568.7, and $572.0\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ . The corresponding specific capacities are 338.9, 316, 393.2, 388.7, 284.4, and 286 C $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Compared with CNF $@$ MOF, C-V@MOF, C-Mo@MOF, C-Mn@MOF, and C-Fe@MOF all exhibit larger specific capacitance, suggesting that V, Mo, Mn and Fe species in CNFs are conductive to improve the specific capacitance of the composites. However, the rate performance of these electrode materials are not very favorable, for example, when the current density increases from 0.5 to 10 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the capacitance retention rate of C-Mn@MOF electrode materials is only $27.80\%$ , which is lower than that of CNF@MOF. The similar results have also been observed after the introduction of V, Mo, Cu and Zn species into CNFs. Their impedance spectra are depicted in Figure 5d to reflect the corresponding resistance characteristics. C-Fe@MOF possesses the low charge transfer resistance and diffusion resistance, suggesting its good electrochemical performance. In terms of capacitive and rate properties, the C-Co@MOF as electrode materials for supercapacitor are superior to C-M@MOF (M=Ni, V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu, and $Z\mathrm{n}$ ). As aforementioned, the high-performance of the C-Co@MOF can be attributed to their uniform and dense modification of MOFs, the accelerated electron transfer from the super graphitization of CNFs, and Co species doping in Ni MOF.
As illustrated in Figure 6a, an asymmetric solid-state supercapacitor (C-Co@MOF//N-CNT ASC) has been constructed to investigate the potential practical application of the as-prepared electrode materials. C-Co@MOF, N-CNT derived from PANI nanotubes, and KOH-PVA are separately selected as the anode materials, cathode materials, and solid-state electrolyte, contributing to a wide voltage window of $1.5\mathrm{~V~}$ for this device. The electrochemical tests of N-CNT electrode are illustrated in Figure S5, which are the basis of charge balance for two electrodes. Figure 6b displays the CV curves at $50\mathrm{mV\thinspaces^{-1}}$ in various voltage windows. According to GCD curves in Figure 6c, the specific capacitance of the device yields 118.3, 90.6, 80.2, 73.4, 66.4, and $61.2\mathrm{F~g^{-1}}$ within the window of 0-1.5, 0-1.4, 0-1.3, 0-1.2, 0-1.1 V measured at $3\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ , respectively, which are achieved using the follow formula.
$$
\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{cell}}{=}(\mathrm{I}{\cdot}\Delta\mathrm{t})/(\mathrm{m}_{\mathrm{total}}{\cdot}\Delta\mathrm{V})
$$
With the increase of scan rates, the shape of CV curves (Figure 6d) maintain essentially unchanged, verifying the nature of fast charge/discharge for capacitive performance of the device. From Figure 6e, the specific capacitance of this device at 5, 7, 10, 13, 15, and $20\mathrm{Ag^{-1}}$ is separately calculated to be 86.1, 63.8, 48.3, 42.7, 39.1, and $28.6\mathrm{F~g^{-1}}$ .
The Ragone plots (energy density vs power density) are provided in Figure 7a, which are obtained in the light of the next formulas.
$\mathrm{{E}}{=}1/2\mathrm{{C}}_{\mathrm{{cell}}}\mathrm{{V}}^{2}$
## $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=E/t}$
E and P refer to the energy density and power density of the device, respectively. The maximum energy density can reach 37.0 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ at $3\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , and the corresponding power density delivers 2250.2 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ , which is higher than many previous asymmetric supercapacitor devices using MOF-based composites as electrode materials, such as, MOF derived Ni-Co-S/activated carbon cloth (ACC)//AC (30.1 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ , 800.2 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ );46 Cu-MOF/Cu $_{2+1}$ O//AC (25.67 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ , 740.44 W kg-1);47 $\mathbf{a}$ -Co/Ni(OH)2@Co $\phantom{0}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}$ -70//AC (23.88 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}},0.075\mathbf{\kW\kg^{-1}})$ ;48 Ni-MOF/rGO-300//AC (17.13 Wh kg-1, 750 W kg-1);18 Porous carbon $\mathrm{\'{Co}}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ derived from G/Co-MOF//AC (21.1 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ , 790 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}.$ );49 and ZIF-67 derived $\mathrm{NiCo_{2}S_{4}//A C}$ (35.3 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ , 750 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ ).50 The large energy density is resulting from the wide potential window and the high specific capacitance of device. When tested at 13 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the device yields a large power density of 9751.8 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ with the energy density of 13.4 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ . Furthermore, C-Co@MOF//N-CNT ASC can light up a red LED for above 5 min when connected in series. As time goes on, the brightness of LED becomes darker, which are reflected in Figure 7b.
Cycle stability and Coulombic efficiency of C-Co $@$ MOF//N-CNT system are carried out to further study its practical usage. As depicted in Figure 7c, after the 6000 cycles, the capacitance retention can maintain above $100\%$ . The increasing of the capacitance may be related to the conversation of C-Co@MOF into hydroxides during the electrochemical process. From the SEM images (Figure S6) and XRD analysis (Figure S7) of C-Co@MOF after cycling tests, it can be noticed that its morphology maintains well, but its crystal structure is transformed into $\mathrm{Co_{x}N i_{l-x}(O H)_{2}}$ , which is consistent with the previous work.42, 51 The large specific surface areas and high redox activities lead to the high capacitance. Meanwhile, the space provided by MOF framework relieves the volume expansion of materials during charging and discharging, which is a vital factor for electrode materials to possess good durability. The Coulombic efficiency of this system ranges from $91.9\%$ to $96.8\%$ , indicating the superior reversibility of redox process.
## Conclusions
In conclusion, we have developed a facile strategy to prepare MOFs grown on Co nanoparticles modified carbon nanofibers (CNFs) with a hierarchical core-shell structure, which shows a superior electrochemical performance over MOFs on Ni, V, Mo, Mn, Fe, Cu and Zn species modified CNFs. Co nanoparicles embedded into CNFs not only enhance the graphitization degree of CNFs, but also provide Co doping in Ni MOF, leading to improved conductivity and pseudocapacitance. The hierarchical core-shell structure combined with porous MOFs offers large surface areas and abundant exposed active sites. As results, C-Co@MOF shows the highest specific capacitance of $1201.6\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ and specific capacity of $600.8\mathrm{~C~g^{-1}}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , which can be retained by $45\%$ when the current density reaches 30 times of the initial value. Furthermore, the C-Co material as anode materials and the CNT derived from PANI as cathode are used to construct an asymmetric solid-state supercapacitor, yielding a maximum energy density of 37.0 Wh $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ at power density of 2250.2 W $\mathbf{kg^{-1}}$ and excellent cycling stability in the course of 6000 charge-discharge cycles. This work presents the ingenious application of metal nanoparticles modified electrospun CNFs and put forwards a new path for the preparation of core-shell metal ions doped MOF based materials, expanding broad promising applications in a wide field.
